When Benjamin opened his eyes, he was greeted by a familiar ceiling and the delicious scent of food. As he sat up in bed, his head felt strangely dull. 'I felt like I had a bad dream...' As he got up, he felt an eerie sensation. Looking out the window, it was heavily overcast and drizzling, making it impossible to tell if it was morning or evening. "Mom." Benjamin called for his mother outside the door, but his voice came out hoarse. His throat felt a bit scratchy. "Breakfast is ready, come and eat. And wake up Uncle Melchizedek." Aisia's voice was heard. He somehow let out a sigh of relief. And then. A single tear fell. 'Huh? Why am I like this? Is it because of the nightmare I had last night?' Benjamin wiped his eyes, got up, tidied the bedsheets as usual, woke up Uncle Melchizedek, and sat around the dining table. "It seems like the smell of food is lingering too much, so it would be good to open the window." Aisia got up from her seat, went to the window, and opened it. "It's a bit cold, as expected." A cold wind blew in through the window. "Mom?" Benjamin, hit by the wind, felt that something was wrong. The cold wind coming through the window gradually turned into a hot wind. Then it suddenly became dark, and the hot wind still felt like it was clawing at his entire body. "Aargh! Mom! It's hot!" He couldn't see anything, and he screamed in pain that enveloped his whole body. From somewhere far away, someone's shouts were heard. "Hold on tight! This one is lucky! Get some water! We might be able to save him." Between the burning sensation all over his body and several dreams, he didn't know which was reality. It was only when he realized the intense thirst and pain that he understood the darkness was reality. He fully returned to reality only when someone unwrapped the bandages from his eyes. The first thing he saw was a doctor with a grim expression. "As expected, half of your body, including your left eye and left ear, will have difficulty functioning properly. But at least you survived." Only then did the events he had considered nightmares in his dream come back to him. "Mom..." "There was a woman who died holding you. The reason you survived, even with half your body intact, was because of her. When the rescue team found you..." "I called for people. Continuously. But they didn't come. Even when my mom was dying..." Benjamin's voice was still hoarse, coming out like a metallic rasp. "It's regrettable." "Regrettable? Is that all?!" Benjamin yelled furiously. The doctor didn't respond further. His expression was neither pity nor scolding. Before Benjamin could grasp the meaning of that inscrutable expression, the doctor uttered a single sentence and got up. "Thousands have died, adults and children alike, and dozens of patients have died in my hands alone. Thank the damn god who saved you. Since you can complain, you can be moved to a bed outside the church." Only then did Benjamin look around. The church was filled with patients teetering between life and death. Benjamin couldn't find anything to say. Just then, Benjamin felt a warm sensation on his hand. "Adel!" What touched his hand was a familiar friend with white fur. He called out the name with joy, but Benjamin's face soon contorted. It wasn't Adel. "Because of you, Mom!" Benjamin raised his hand and hit the baby ferret. The ferret shrieked and hid somewhere. "Come out, you! Mom died because of you!" To find the escaped ferret, Benjamin got down from the bed and took a step. However, he screamed and collapsed from the burning sensation coming from one foot. An old nun heard the scream and rushed over. "What's wrong? Why did you get out of bed? You're not well enough to get up yet, so lie back down. You're quite a handful, child." The nun grumbled and put Benjamin back on the bed. Thinking that if he hadn't gone back home to find the baby ferret, this wouldn't have happened, anger welled up in him, and he huffed and puffed, searching for it while lying down, but the ferret did not reappear before Benjamin's eyes.

Lying on the bed and looking around, everything was unfamiliar. Unknown scenery, unknown people. No one offered a warm gaze. After a long time, the thought of being alone finally washed over him. "Hmph. Mom..." Lying on the bed, he covered his face with one arm and cried. His tear ducts stung from the burns. As the pain was felt, sorrow welled up. "Waaah." "Looks like you're finally awake. Lucky kid." At the sudden voice, he wiped his tears and raised his head. A young woman stood in front of the bed, holding a basket. "Last time I came, you seemed hopeless, but seeing you even crying, you must be doing better. I'm a healing mage from the kingdom. If you're awake, you must be hungry, so have some of this. Healing magic basically accelerates a person's natural healing ability, so it's ineffective if you don't have energy." The woman sat beside Benjamin's bed and took out some food from the basket. As the woman took out the food, the ferret that had disappeared reappeared. "Oh my. I wondered where it went. It was still here. Is this your friend?" "......." Benjamin felt rather flustered by her sudden kindness and didn't answer. She cut a piece of bread and gave it to the ferret. The ferret glanced at Benjamin, then began to eat the bread. "What do you mean by 'lucky kid'?" Benjamin, annoyed by the epithet used for him, asked back. "You're lucky in many ways. You were the only one alive so close to the epicenter. This ferret led to your discovery almost at the end of the survivor search. Immediately after rescue, your condition was very poor, and your survival was uncertain. Of course, you could have regained consciousness with recovery magic, but right now, we're not in a situation to pour a lot of magic power into one person. But you regained consciousness in a few days, and so, to prevent your condition from worsening again, the use of magic power on you was authorized. So, I can only say you're lucky." Once the ferret was somewhat full, it tried to approach Benjamin again. When Benjamin raised his hand with a scary face, the ferret scurried behind the woman. The young female mage frowned at Benjamin's behavior and scolded him. "What are you doing to a small animal? This one was always by your side while you were unconscious. And if it weren't for this one, you wouldn't have even been found. Besides, this one seems to be young too." "Because of this one..." Benjamin tried to make an excuse but couldn't continue. Benjamin knew well that none of this was the ferret's fault. Even if he hadn't gone back home to find the missing ferret. Looking back, he realized he had done something incredibly absurd. In fact, it was no one's fault. No one knew such a fiery thunderbolt would fall from the sky. It wasn't this little mischief-maker's fault. But his mother had died, and Benjamin needed to find a reason he could accept. Now, the only one he knew from before the accident was this small creature. Both the ferret and Benjamin were now alone, except for each other. As that thought came to him, he noticed the baby ferret's earnest gaze, wanting to come closer despite being afraid of him. It too had lost its mother Adel and its siblings. "What's this little friend's name?" The mage asked. Benjamin was silent for a while, staring intently at the baby ferret. "Adel." Benjamin didn't know why he answered with its mother's name. Was it because he needed his old friend Adel now? Come to think of it, this one resembled Adel the most among the litter. As soon as Benjamin uttered its mother's name, the baby ferret scurried over as if it had been waiting, and rubbed its face against Benjamin's hand. "I see. That's a good name. Let's start the treatment then. This one treatment will make a noticeable difference. I can only do this kind of treatment for two people a day. You really are lucky." Benjamin stroked the head of the baby ferret, now named Adel, with one hand, and as her treatment began, Benjamin felt his body becoming more comfortable.

